  "Number": 5,
  "Question": "Two positive numbers \\(x\\) and \\(y\\) are in the ratio \\(a:b,\\) where \\(0\\lt a\\lt b.\\) If \\(x+y=c,\\) then the smaller of \\(x\\) and \\(y\\) is",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(\\frac{ac}{b}\\)",
    "B": "\\(\\frac{bc-ac}{b}\\)",
    "C": "\\(\\frac{ac}{a+b}\\)",
    "D": "\\(\\frac{bc}{a+b}\\)",
    "E": "\\(\\frac{ac}{b-a}\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "C",
  "Solution": "The ratio \\(x:y=a:b\\) means that \\(x=at\\) and \\(y=bt\\) for some positive scale factor \\(t\\). Since \\(a<b\\) and \\(t>0\\), we have \\(x<y\\).\n\nThe sum condition gives\n\\[(a+b)t=c.\\]\nBecause \\(a+b>0\\), we can divide to obtain \\(t=c/(a+b)\\). Thus the smaller number is\n\\[x=at=\\frac{ac}{a+b},\\]\nwhich is choice C.",
